          "review_status": "extracted_needs_review",
          "exact_quote": "chapter 1\ncharacterIsatIon and \npurIfIcatIon of cheMIcal \nsubstances\nexperIMent 1.1\nTo determine the boiling point of an organic compound.\napparatus requIred\nBeaker (50 mL), capillary tube, ignition tube, thermometer, \niron stand (with clamp), wire gauze, tripod stand and burner/\nspirit lamp.\ncheMIcals requIred\norganic liquids, such as \nBenzene and Ethyl methyl \nketone for boiling point \n(b.p.) determination.\nprocedure\n 1. Take a 50 mL glass \nbeaker and fill it \nhalf with water.\n 2. Take a capillary \ntube and seal its \none end by heating \nit\tin\tthe\tflame.\n 3. Transfer a few \ndrops of the organic \nliquid compound in \nthe ignition tube. \nDip the open end of \ncapillary tube into \nthe liquid taken in \nthe ignition tube.\nFig. 1.1: Arrangement showing \nboiling point determination\nChemistry Manual Final _31-10-2017.indd   17 19-03-2018   14:21:49"
        },
        {
          "passage_id": "Manual_01:fc4dcbac1a60:p0029",
